      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-104317",
      ":question/problem": "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,\u03d5),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,\u03d5)=\u27e8\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $\u27e8z,r\u27e9=\u27e8k\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5,k\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9$* for $\u03d5\\in R.$"
